<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Syrian Wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">274–<a href="/years/168-bce">168 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Syrian Wars were a series of conflicts fought between the Ptolemaic and Seleucid kingdoms during the third and second centuries BCE. They centred on control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coele-syria">Coele-Syria</a>, the strategically and commercially valuable territory between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Empire</a> identify them as the kingdom's key military conflicts, which drained resources from both Hellenistic dynasties.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/syrian-wars.jpg" alt="Syrian Wars" width="316" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA, earthobservatory · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Near_east_lrg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The wars were fought between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Kingdom</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, successor states to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s empire, over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coele-syria">Coele-Syria</a>, now south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beqaa-valley">Beqaa Valley</a>. The corridor linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> to Egypt, threatened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>, lay within the natural continuation of Seleucid Syria and yielded substantial agricultural and commercial revenue. After the Wars of the Diadochi Ptolemy I Soter took the region in 301 BCE, exploiting events around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ipsus">Battle of Ipsus</a>, although the victors there had assigned it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a>. Seleucus, who had been helped by Ptolemy, did not try to reclaim it, and war began once both were dead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In the First Syrian War (274–271 BCE) Antiochus I took Ptolemaic areas in coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and Ptolemy II reconquered them by 271 BCE. The Second (260–253 BCE) saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-ii-theos">Antiochus II</a>, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-ii-gonatas">Antigonus II Gonatas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, regain Miletus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, and ended around 253 BCE with his marriage to Ptolemy's daughter Berenice Syra. The Third, or Laodicean, War (246–241 BCE) followed a succession dispute at the Seleucid court. Ptolemy III occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and even reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, and in 241 BCE he gained Seleucia Pieria, the port of Antioch, when the Ptolemaic kingdom was at its height.</p><p>In the Fourth War (219–217 BCE) <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> retook Seleucia Pieria and cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy IV Philopator</a> defeated him at Raphia in summer 217 BCE, using at least thirty thousand native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> as phalangites, which later fed the Egyptian Revolt. In the Fifth (202–195 BCE) Antiochus defeated the Ptolemies at Panium and completed the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coele-syria">Coele-Syria</a> in 198 BCE, and in 195 BCE Ptolemy V made peace and agreed to marry Antiochus's daughter Cleopatra I. In the Sixth (170–168 BCE) <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iv-epiphanes">Antiochus IV Epiphanes</a> defeated the Ptolemaic army in the Sinai, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelusium">Pelusium</a> and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In 168 BCE, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleusis">Eleusis</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, the Roman envoy Gaius Popillius Laenas gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iv-epiphanes">Antiochus IV</a> an ultimatum from the Senate to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> he drew a circle in the sand round the king and told him to decide before stepping out of it. Antiochus obeyed, which ended the Sixth War. The conflicts drained the resources and manpower of both kingdoms and led to their eventual conquest by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>. In 145 BCE Ptolemy VI intervened in the Seleucid civil war and won the Battle of the Oenoparus, but died of his wounds, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-ii-nicator">Demetrius II</a> drove the Ptolemaic force out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coele-syria">Coele-Syria</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Syrian Wars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The principal conflicts were the <b>Syrian Wars</b> against the Seleucid Empire, fought over Coele-Syria and other strategic territories.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
